     1	# phys-bilinear-higher-dim-integrability
     2	
     3	Book problem: 关于得到高维可积模型的一个新猜想 / "On a new conjecture to find higher-dimensional integrable
     4	models", Lou Senyue and Li Jinhua (Ningbo University), 《10000个科学难题·物理学卷》 (Science Press, 2009),
     5	book pp. 350–351 (PDF pp. 383–384).
     6	
     7	## Summary of the book problem
     8	
     9	Lou and Li conjecture a "holographic" route from (1+1)-dimensional to higher-dimensional integrable models in
    10	Hirota's bilinear formalism: if the bilinear system `P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0, Q(D_x)P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0` is
    11	integrable, then `P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0, R(S)P(D_x, D_t)F·G = 0` may be integrable, where S is the Hirota
    12	operator of an extra (continuous, discrete, supersymmetric or q-deformed) variable and R is a polynomial,
    13	often of the same form as Q. The printed `D_c` in the second system is a typo for `D_t` (STATUS.md §1). The
    14	book asks how to prove the conjecture, how to determine R(S), and which extra conditions make it true if it is
    15	false. The cited source (Li & Lou, Chin. Phys. B 17, 747 (2008)) proves three examples by symmetry methods
    16	and leaves open whether `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` is integrable for other n.
    17	
    18	## Formulations attacked
    19	
    20	"Integrable" is made checkable as Hirota's multi-soliton property (RESULTS.md §0):
    21	
    22	- **(R-sys)**, literal: with Q = D_x, R = D_y (any linear Q, R reduces to this), if the two-function system (1)
    23	  passes the generic N-soliton test (all one-soliton seeds, independent amplitudes in F and G), so does (2).
    24	  Studied in the class 𝒫: `P = D_t + p(D_x)`, `p(0) = 0`, `deg p ≥ 2`, which contains Li & Lou's family.
    25	- **(R-eq)**, single-function: the F = G reduction, `Q(D_x)P f·f = 0` integrable ⇒ `Q(D_y)P f·f = 0`
    26	  integrable (P, Q odd).
    27	
    28	Not covered: models whose integrability is invisible to exponential solitons (e.g. `P = D_x D_t`),
    29	supersymmetric and q-deformed S.
    30	
    31	## Verdict
    32	
    33	**PARTIAL** for the literal conjecture and **COUNTEREXAMPLE** for its single-function reading.
    34	FRONTIER state: PROOF-OBLIGATION, NOVELTY-UNCLEAR.
    35	
    36	- In 𝒫 the literal conjecture holds at the three-soliton level: (1) passes ⇔ (2) passes ⇔ `deg p ≤ 3`
    37	  (Theorem 4.4). In particular Li & Lou's system passes only for n = 2, 3; for n ≥ 4 it has no generic
    38	  three-soliton solutions. The same rule works with the discrete operator `R(S) = sinh(D_n)`.
    39	- The single-function reading is false: `D_x(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0` (Sawada–Kotera) is integrable, but
    40	  `D_y(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0` fails Hirota's three-soliton condition; the obstruction (proportional to `c5`) is
    41	  computed exactly and reproduces the constraint found by Wang et al. (2024).
    42	- So the extra condition asked for by the book is, in 𝒫: require integrability of (1) as a genuine
    43	  two-component system (independent F and G amplitudes), not only on its F = G reduction.
    44	- Open: the N-soliton property for all N in the passing class (N = 4, 5 checked; see RESULTS.md C6), and
    45	  P outside 𝒫 (an exploratory scan found no counterexample).
