     7	
     8	## 1. What the book states
     9	
    10	If the (1+1)-dimensional bilinear system
    11	
    12	    (1)  P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0,   Q(D_x) P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0
    13	
    14	is integrable, then
    15	
    16	    (2)  P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0,   R(S) P(D_x, D_t) F·G = 0
    17	
    18	"may be" (可能是) integrable. P and Q are polynomials, R is an undetermined polynomial ("in many cases R has
    19	the same functional form as Q"), and S may be the Hirota operator of an extra dimension, a supersymmetric,
    20	discrete or q-deformed Hirota operator. The book says that Ref. [6] (Li & Lou 2008) gives seven simple
    21	successful examples, all with R and Q of the same linear form and R(S) = D_y or R(S) = A (a supersymmetric
    22	bilinear operator). It asks: how to prove the conjecture, how to determine R(S), and, if it fails, which extra
    23	conditions make it hold.
    24	
    25	Typography. The page image (`source/loulipage-384.png`) prints the second equation of (2) as
    26	`R(S)P(D_x, D_c)F,G = 0`. Both primary statements of the same ansatz write `P(D_t, D_x)` in both equations
    27	(Li & Lou 2008, Eqs. (62)–(63); Wang, Lou & Qian 2010, Eqs. (4)–(5)), so `D_c` is a typo for `D_t`, and
    28	`F,G` denotes the bilinear pair F·G.
    29	
    30	Precision. The book's statement is a heuristic: it does not define "integrable" and does not say whether
    31	integrability of (1) is meant for general (F, G) or only on special reductions such as F = G. The precise
    32	readings used here are fixed in `RESULTS.md` §0.
    33	
    34	## 2. Primary sources on the conjecture
    35	
    36	| Source | What it contains | Access |
    37	|---|---|---|
    38	| Li Jin-Hua, Lou Sen-Yue, "Kac–Moody–Virasoro symmetry algebra of a (2+1)-dimensional bilinear system", *Chin. Phys. B* **17**(3), 747–753 (2008), doi:[10.1088/1674-1056/17/3/002](https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1056/17/3/002) | Book's Ref. [6]. Three explicit models: (i) `D_y D_x D_t F·G = 0, D_x D_t F·G = 0` (bilinear form of the isotropic integrable sine-Gordon / Loewner–Konopelchenko–Rogers system); (ii) `(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0` (a subcase of the bilinear non-isotropic sine/sinh-Gordon system); (iii) `(D_t + D_x^3)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^3)F·G = 0`, shown to have infinitely many generalized symmetries (formal series symmetry approach) and a Kac–Moody–Virasoro point-symmetry algebra. Asks (their Eq. (7)) whether `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` is integrable for other n, and states the ansatz (62)–(63). | Full text read: CPB site PDF (`https://cpb.iphy.ac.cn/en/article/pdf/preview/10.1088/1674-1056/17/3/002.pdf`, sha256 `9186c7bf…a739d`) |
    39	| Wang You-Fa, Lou Sen-Yue, Qian Xian-Min, "A new (2+1)-dimensional supersymmetric Boussinesq equation and its Lie symmetry study", *Chin. Phys. B* **19**(5), 050202 (2010), doi:[10.1088/1674-1056/19/5/050202](https://doi.org/10.1088/1674-1056/19/5/050202) | Restates the conjecture ("Conjecture: If (4) is integrable, then (5) may be integrable … in many cases R(x) = Q(x)"), lists the three Li–Lou models "and some others in Refs. [17]–[19] and [20]" (supersymmetric bilinear forms of Liu, Hu, Zhang, Wang, Wu, Yang 2005–2008). Proposes `(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0, S_y(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0` (super-Hirota S_y) and calls it "a further positive example of the conjecture", adding that "the conjecture should be checked further". | Full text read (CPB site PDF) |
    40	| Wang Jianyong, Yang Yunqing, Tang Xiaoyan, Chen Yong, "A novel (2+1)-dimensional Sawada–Kotera type system: multisoliton solution and variable separation solution", *Nonlinear Dyn.* (2024), doi:[10.1007/s11071-024-09511-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/s11071-024-09511-0); preprint Research Square rs-3801786 (CC BY 4.0) | Studies `D_y(D_x^5 + D_t) f·f = 0`, obtained from the Sawada–Kotera bilinear form `D_x(D_x^5 + D_t) f·f = 0` "only by changing one of the Hirota operators D_x → D_y". Finds three- and four-soliton solutions only under extra constraints on the wave numbers (their Eq. (16) for N = 3) and conjectures N-soliton solutions under such constraints. Does not discuss the Li–Lou conjecture. | Preprint full text read (sha256 `b99addf5…d966c`); journal version not read |
