    57	bilinear equations passing Hirota's three-soliton condition. I. KdV-type bilinear equations", *J. Math. Phys.*
    58	**28**, 1732 (1987) (the three-soliton test as an integrability criterion); K. Sawada, T. Kotera, *Prog.
    59	Theor. Phys.* **51**, 1355 (1974) (N-soliton solutions of the Sawada–Kotera equation, bilinear form
    60	`D_x(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0`). Hietarinta's papers II–IV (mKdV-, sine-Gordon- and complex-type bilinear
    61	equations) were **not** read; their classes have a different second equation and are not known to us to
    62	cover the systems (1)/(2) with the same pair F·G in both equations (NOVELTY-UNCLEAR, see RESULTS.md).
    63	
    64	## 3. Which seven examples?
    65	
    66	The cited Ref. [6] (Li & Lou 2008) contains three explicit (2+1)-dimensional examples, (i)–(iii) above, each
    67	paired with its (1+1)-dimensional counterpart Q = D_x. It contains no supersymmetric example. The seven
    68	examples with "R(S) = D_y or R(S) = A" therefore cannot be listed exactly from the cited paper. Wang, Lou &
    69	Qian (2010) attribute further examples to the supersymmetric bilinear literature (their Refs. [17]–[20]); from
    70	that paper the supersymmetric Boussinesq pair `(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0, S_x(D_t + D_x^2)F·G = 0` and its
    71	`S_y` extension are explicit. Identifying the remaining examples with the supersymmetric KdV/mKdV/two-boson
    72	bilinear forms of Liu, Hu and co-workers is an inference, not checked against those papers.
    73	
    74	## 4. Settled vs open (2026-10-14)
    75	
    76	Settled in the literature:
    77	
    78	- Positive examples: the three Li–Lou models and the Wang–Lou–Qian supersymmetric model are shown
    79	  "integrable" in the sense of infinitely many (generalized or Lie point) symmetries. Neither of these two
    80	  papers constructs general N-soliton solutions of the two-function systems (citing papers that we did not
    81	  read in full may contain such solutions).
    82	- The single-function (F = G) version of the n = 5 case, `D_y(D_t + D_x^5) f·f = 0`, has three- and
    83	  four-soliton solutions only under wave-number constraints (Wang et al. 2024, by explicit computation). The
    84	  generic three-soliton failure is implied by their need for constraint (16) but not stated as a theorem,
    85	  and the paper does not connect it to the Li–Lou conjecture.
    86	
    87	Explicitly open in the primary sources:
    88	
    89	- Li & Lou 2008, Section 4: "Because of complicity of calculation, we have not yet found other integrable
    90	  systems from Eq. (7)" — i.e. whether `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` is integrable for any
    91	  n other than 2, 3 — and "Even if the ansatz is not correct, we believe that we can still obtain many new
    92	  integrable models from the ansatz!"
    93	- Wang, Lou & Qian 2010: "the conjecture should be checked further".
    94	
    95	No proof, general counterexample or class-level characterization of the conjecture was found (searches of
    96	the web, OpenAlex citations of Li & Lou 2008, arXiv). An unsuccessful search does not establish that none
    97	exists.
    98	
    99	## 5. Baseline to beat
   100	
   101	- Strongest positive evidence: three continuous-variable examples (`P = D_x D_t`, `D_t + D_x^2`,
   102	  `D_t + D_x^3`, each with Q = D_x and R = D_y) and one supersymmetric example, each verified only by symmetry
   103	  analysis, one model at a time.
   104	- Strongest negative evidence: the Wang et al. (2024) constrained-soliton computation for the F = G reduction
   105	  of n = 5, not framed as evidence against the conjecture.
   106	- No class of (P, Q) is known for which the conjecture is proved, and no rule for R(S) beyond R = Q.
