    10	
    11	## 0. Precise formulations
    12	
    13	**Hirota operators.** For a polynomial `B(D_x, D_y, D_t)` and wave vectors `κ = (k, l, w)`,
    14	`B(D) e^{κ·z}·e^{κ'·z} = B(κ − κ') e^{(κ+κ')·z}`, `z = (x, y, t)`. Hence for
    15	`F = Σ_S F_S e^{η_S}`, `G = Σ_S G_S e^{η_S}` (`S ⊆ {1..N}`, `η_S = Σ_{i∈S} η_i`, `η_i = κ_i·z + const`) the
    16	coefficient of `e^{Σ n_i η_i}`, `n ∈ {0,1,2}^N`, in `B(D)F·G` is `C_B(n) = Σ_{μ+ν=n} F_μ G_ν B(κ_μ − κ_ν)`.
    17	
    18	**Systems.** `P` is a polynomial in `(D_x, D_t)` with `P(0) = 0`.
    19	
    20	    (1)  P F·G = 0,  D_x P F·G = 0          (2)  P F·G = 0,  D_y P F·G = 0
    21	
    22	A linear `Q = αD_x + β` (α ≠ 0) gives the same system as `Q = D_x`, because the second equation equals
    23	`α D_x P F·G + β P F·G` and the first equation removes the β-term; likewise for `R`. So "Q and R of the
    24	same linear form" means Q = D_x, R = D_y.
    25	
    26	**The class 𝒫.** `P = D_t + p(D_x)`, `p` a polynomial with `p(0) = 0` and `deg p ≥ 2`. It contains Li & Lou's
    27	family (7) (`p = k^n`) and their models (ii) n = 2 and (iii) n = 3. It does not contain their model (i),
    28	`P = D_x D_t`.
    29	
    30	**One-soliton seeds.** `F = 1 + a e^{η}`, `G = 1 + b e^{η}` solves (1) or (2) iff
    31	`P(κ)a + P(−κ)b = 0` and `M(κ)[P(κ)a − P(−κ)b] = 0`, where `M(κ) = k` for (1) and `M(κ) = l` for (2).
    32	For `M(κ) ≠ 0` this means `P(κ)a = 0 = P(−κ)b`. For `P ∈ 𝒫` a seed is therefore of
    33	
    34	- F type: `a ≠ 0, b = 0, w = −p(k)`;
    35	- G type: `a = 0, b ≠ 0, w = p(−k)`;
    36	- two-amplitude type (a, b free): only if `p(k) + p(−k) = 0`, i.e. for all k iff p is odd.
    37	
    38	**Generic N-soliton test (two-function systems).** Fix N seeds of given types with generic parameters
    39	(`k_i`, for (2) also `l_i`, amplitudes). For every `S` with `|S| ≥ 2`, the two equations `C_{B_1}(1_S) =
    40	C_{B_2}(1_S) = 0` (`B_1 = P`, `B_2 = MP`) are linear in `(F_S, G_S)` with determinant
    41	`−2 M(κ_S) P(κ_S) P(−κ_S)`; when it is nonzero they fix `F_S, G_S` uniquely. The system *passes the test for
    42	a type pattern* if then every coefficient `C_{B_j}(n)`, `n ∈ {0,1,2}^N`, vanishes identically as a rational
    43	function of the free parameters. It *passes the generic N-soliton test* if it passes for every type pattern
    44	(for odd p: two free amplitudes per soliton, which contains all F/G patterns as specialisations). The
    45	**three-soliton test (3ST)** is N = 3. Failing means: for generic parameters there is no solution of the
    46	form above with these seeds.
    47	
    48	**Single-function test.** For even `B`, Hirota's ansatz `f = Σ_S Π_{i<j∈S} A_ij e^{η_S}`,
    49	`A_ij = −B(κ_i − κ_j)/B(κ_i + κ_j)`, `B(κ_i) = 0`; `B(D)f·f = 0` passes the 3ST if all coefficients vanish
    50	identically.
    51	
    52	**Readings of the conjecture.**
    53	
    54	- **(R-sys)**, the literal reading: if (1) passes the generic N-soliton test for every N, so does (2).
    55	  **(R-sys-3)**: the same statement with N = 3 only.
    56	- **(R-eq)**, the single-function reading: for odd P and odd Q, if `Q(D_x)P f·f = 0` is integrable then
    57	  `Q(D_y)P f·f = 0` is integrable. Because `P f·f ≡ 0` for odd P, these are exactly the F = G reductions of
    58	  (1) and (2). Here "integrable" for the premise is taken in any of the usual strong senses (Lax pair and
    59	  N-soliton solutions for all N); the conclusion is refuted by failure of the 3ST.
    60	
    61	**What these readings do not cover.** Integrability that is invisible to exponential solitons (for
    62	`P = D_x D_t` the seed conditions force `kw = 0`, so this test is degenerate); integrability defined by Lax
    63	pairs or symmetries alone; supersymmetric and q-deformed operators S. The discrete case is touched in C7.
