    77	
    78	## 2. Statements and proofs
    79	
    80	### C1. Diagonal reduction
    81	
    82	*Claim.* If `(F, G)(x, y, t) = (F̃, G̃)(x + y, t)` solves (2), then `(F̃, G̃)(x, t)` solves (1). Consequently,
    83	if (2) passes the generic N-soliton test for a type pattern, so does (1).
    84	
    85	*Proof.* For functions of `s = x + y` and t, `∂_y = ∂_x = ∂_s`, so `D_y P(D_x, D_t)F·G = D_x P(D_x, D_t)F·G`,
    86	both evaluated at `s`; the first equations coincide. For the test: the seed conditions do not involve M
    87	(for `M(κ) ≠ 0`), so seeds of (2) with `l_i = k_i` are seeds of (1), and the recursion and residuals of (2) at
    88	`l_i = k_i` are literally those of (1) (the multiplier `λ_μ − λ_ν` becomes `k_μ − k_ν`). The residuals of (2)
    89	are zero rational functions; the specialisation `l = k` keeps the determinants `−2 k_S P(κ_S)P(−κ_S)`
    90	nonzero polynomials, so the residuals of (1) vanish identically. ∎
    91	
    92	### C2. The F = G sector
    93	
    94	*Claim.* Let P be odd and `M ∈ {D_x, D_y}`. With seeds `a_i = b_i`, the recursion gives `F_S = G_S` for all S,
    95	and these coefficients are those of the single-function recursion for `MP f·f = 0`. For N = 3 the
    96	single-function recursion passes iff Hirota's product ansatz passes.
    97	
    98	*Proof.* Induction on |S|. If `F_T = G_T` for `|T| < |S|`, the first equation at `n = 1_S` reads
    99	`P(κ_S)(F_S − G_S) + Σ_{T≠∅,S} F_T F_{S∖T} P(κ_T − κ_{S∖T}) = 0`; the sum vanishes because the terms `T` and
   100	`S∖T` cancel (P odd), so `F_S = G_S` (as `P(κ_S) ≠ 0`). The second equation is then the single-function
   101	equation for `B = MP`. For N = 3 the single-function residual at `n = (2,1,1)` equals
   102	`2[f_1 f_123 B(κ_2+κ_3) + f_12 f_13 B(κ_2−κ_3)]`, which vanishes iff `f_123 = A_12A_13A_23 f_1f_2f_3`
   103	(given `f_1 B(κ_2+κ_3) ≠ 0`), i.e. iff the product ansatz satisfies the `(1,1,1)` equation, which is
   104	Hirota's condition; for the product ansatz all coefficients with an entry 2 vanish automatically. ∎
   105	
   106	So if `MP f·f = 0` fails the 3ST, the two-function system fails the 3ST (at `a_i = b_i`, hence generically).
   107	
   108	### C3. `deg p ≤ 3` passes (both systems)
   109	
   110	`two_function_3ss.py`, Part A, runs the two-function recursion in the field
   111	`ℚ(k_i, l_i, a_i, b_i, c_1, c_2, c_3)` and finds every residual coefficient identically zero for
   112	
   113	- `p = c1 k + c3 k^3` with two free amplitudes per soliton, for (1) and for (2);
   114	- `p = c1 k + c2 k^2 + c3 k^3` and `p = c1 k + c2 k^2`, each of the 8 F/G patterns, for (1) and (2);
   115	- the specialisations `k^3`, `k^3 − 2k` (two-amplitude) and `k^2`, `k^3 + k^2`, `3k^2 + k` (all patterns).
   116	
   117	Printed: `Part A: 86 symbolic three-soliton identities, nonzero residual sets: none`.
   118	
   119	Specialisation to any particular p with `deg p ∈ {2, 3}` is legitimate: the only denominators are the
   120	determinants `−2M(κ_S)P(κ_S)P(−κ_S)`, and for `deg p = d ≥ 2` each `P(±κ_S)`, `|S| ≥ 2`, is a nonzero
   121	polynomial in the `k_i`: its top-degree part `c_d[(Σ_{i∈S} ε_i k_i)^d − Σ_{i∈S} ε_i σ_i k_i^d]` (`σ_i = ±1`
   122	from the seed type) contains, for `i ≠ j ∈ S`, the monomial `k_i^{d−1} k_j` with coefficient `±d c_d ≠ 0`.
   123	
   124	### C4. `deg p ≥ 4` fails (both systems); classification in 𝒫
   125	
   126	**Lemma 4.1 (F F G pattern).** Let P be any polynomial with `P(0) = 0` and `M ∈ {D_x, D_y}`, with values
   127	`m_i = M(κ_i)` (`k_i` or `l_i`). Take solitons 1, 2 of F type (`P(κ_1) = P(κ_2) = 0`, `b_1 = b_2 = 0`) and
   128	soliton 3 of G type (`P(−κ_3) = 0`, `a_3 = 0`). Assume `m_S ≠ 0` and `P(±κ_S) ≠ 0` for
   129	`S ∈ {12, 13, 23, 123}`. Then the coefficient of `e^{2η_1+η_2+η_3}` in `P(D)F·G` equals
   130	
   131	    a_1^2 a_2 b_3 m_3 Φ / ( m_123 P(−κ_123) P(−κ_13) ),
   132	    Φ = P(κ_2−κ_3) P(κ_1−κ_2−κ_3) P(−κ_1−κ_3) + P(κ_1−κ_3) P(κ_2−κ_1−κ_3) P(−κ_2−κ_3).
   133	
   134	In particular, if `Φ ≠ 0` the system fails the 3ST, and this criterion does not depend on M.
   135	
   136	*Proof.* Level 12: the lower terms `F_1G_2`, `F_2G_1` vanish, so `F_12 = G_12 = 0`. Level 13 (only `F_1G_3`
   137	contributes): `F_13P(κ_13) + G_13P(−κ_13) = −a_1b_3P(κ_1−κ_3)` and
   138	`m_13[F_13P(κ_13) − G_13P(−κ_13)] = −a_1b_3 (m_1−m_3) P(κ_1−κ_3)`; subtracting and using
   139	`m_13 − (m_1 − m_3) = 2m_3` gives `G_13 P(−κ_13) = −a_1b_3 P(κ_1−κ_3) m_3/m_13`. Likewise
   140	`G_23P(−κ_23) = −a_2b_3P(κ_2−κ_3)m_3/m_23`.
   141	Level 123: only `T = 1` (`a_1G_23`) and `T = 2` (`a_2G_13`) contribute besides `F_123, G_123`. With
   142	`X = a_1G_23P(κ_1−κ_2−κ_3)`, `Y = a_2G_13P(κ_2−κ_1−κ_3)` the two equations give
   143	`G_123P(−κ_123) = −[X m_23 + Y m_13]/m_123`, i.e.
   144	`G_123 = a_1a_2b_3 m_3 Φ / (m_123 P(−κ_123)P(−κ_13)P(−κ_23))`. At `n = (2,1,1)` the pairs are
   145	`(123, 1)` (contains `G_1 = 0`), `(12, 13)`, `(13, 12)` (contain `F_12 = G_12 = 0`) and `(1, 123)`, so the
   146	coefficient is `a_1G_123P(−κ_23)`. ∎
   147	
   148	`two_function_3ss.py`, Part B, re-derives this identity with every value `P(Σ ε_iκ_i)` an independent symbol
   149	(only the dispersion relations imposed) and arbitrary `m_i`: printed `closed form ... True`.
   150	
   151	**Lemma 4.2 (monomials).** Let `p = k^d` and `(k_1, k_2, k_3) = (1, 2, −4)` with types F, F, G, so
   152	`w = (−1, −2^d, 4^d)`. Then
   153	
   154	    Φ_d = (6^d − 4^d − 2^d)(3^d + 2^d − 4^d − 1)(3^d + 1 − 4^d) + (5^d − 4^d − 1)(5^d − 4^d − 2^d + 1)(2^{d+1} − 4^d),
   155	
   156	`Φ_2 = Φ_3 = 0` and `Φ_d < 0` for every `d ≥ 4` (`Φ_4 = −672768`). The hypotheses of Lemma 4.1 hold for all
   157	`d ≥ 2`, for (1) (`k_12, k_13, k_23, k_123 = 3, −3, −2, −1`) and for (2) with `l = (1, 3, 7)`.
   158	
   159	*Proof.* `4 ≤ d ≤ 60`: exact integer evaluation (Part C). `d ≥ 30`: the first product is positive and
   160	`< 6^d·4^d·4^d = 96^d` (its last two factors are negative with absolute values `< 4^d`). In the second product,
   161	for `d ≥ 4` the first two factors are `≥ 5^d − 2·4^d > 0` and the last is `≤ −4^d/2`, so it is
   162	`≤ −100^d (1 − 2·0.8^d)^2 / 2`. Hence `Φ_d < 0` whenever `(1 − 2·0.8^d)^2 ≥ 2·0.96^d`; this holds at d = 30
   163	(checked exactly) and the left side increases and the right side decreases with d. Nondegeneracy:
   164	`P(κ_12) = 3^d − 2^d − 1 > 0`; `P(−κ_12) = 1 + 2^d + (−3)^d ≠ 0`; `P(κ_13) = 4^d − 1 + (−3)^d > 0`;
   165	`P(−κ_13) = 1 + 3^d − 4^d < 0`; `P(κ_23) = 4^d − 2^d + (−2)^d > 0`; `P(−κ_23) = 2^{d+1} − 4^d < 0`;
   166	`P(κ_123) = 4^d − 2^d − 1 + (−1)^d > 0`; `P(−κ_123) = 2 + 2^d − 4^d < 0` (all for `d ≥ 2`, `d ≥ 3` where
   167	needed); Part C rechecks them for `d ≤ 60`. ∎
   168	
   169	Part C also recomputes the full recursion for `d = 4, …, 9` for (1) and for (2) and confirms that the engine's
   170	`(2,1,1)` residual equals the closed form and is nonzero.
   171	
   172	**Lemma 4.3 (lower-order terms).** Let `deg p = d ≥ 4` with leading coefficient `c_d`. At the configuration
   173	`s·(1, 2, −4)` (and `l = (1, 3, 7)` for (2)), each `P(±κ_S)` and each factor of Φ is a polynomial in s of
   174	degree ≤ d whose `s^d` coefficient is `c_d` times the corresponding monomial value of Lemma 4.2. Hence
   175	`Φ(s)` has `s^{3d}` coefficient `c_d^3 Φ_d ≠ 0`, every hypothesis of Lemma 4.1 holds for all but finitely many
   176	s, and the 3ST fails. (Part D shows nonzero residuals for `k^5+k^2`, `k^4+k^3`, `k^6−3k^2+k`, `2k^5−k^3+k`,
   177	`k^7+k^5+k^3` at s = 1, 2, 5, 20.) ∎
   178	
   179	**Theorem 4.4 (classification in 𝒫).** For `P = D_t + p(D_x)`, `p(0) = 0`, `deg p ≥ 2`, the following are
   180	equivalent: (a) (1) passes the generic 3ST; (b) (2) passes it; (c) `deg p ≤ 3`.
   181	
   182	*Proof.* (c)⇒(b): C3. (b)⇒(a): C1. (a)⇒(c): if `deg p ≥ 4`, Lemmas 4.1–4.3 with `M = D_x` give an
   183	F F G configuration with nonzero residual; F- and G-type seeds exist for every p, and for odd p they are
   184	specialisations of the two-amplitude seeds. ∎ (Lemmas 4.1–4.3 with `M = D_y` show directly that (2) fails.)
   185	
   186	**Corollaries.**
   187	
   188	- *Li–Lou question (7).* `(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0, D_y(D_t + D_x^n)F·G = 0` passes the generic 3ST iff
   189	  n ∈ {2, 3} (n = 1 is linear and degenerate). For n ≥ 4 neither this system nor its (1+1)-dimensional
   190	  counterpart admits generic three-soliton solutions, so in the Hirota multi-soliton sense no further n gives
   191	  an integrable model. This answers the question left open in Li & Lou (2008), Section 4, negatively in that
   192	  sense; it says nothing about symmetry integrability.
   193	- *(R-sys-3) holds in 𝒫* with `R(S) = Q(D_y) = D_y`: the hypothesis and the conclusion hold for exactly the
   194	  same P. The all-N statement (R-sys) in 𝒫 reduces to the open obligation in C6.
   195	
   196	### C5. The single-function reading (R-eq) is false
   197	
